export abstract anchor FactoryValidation:
    activation:
        Activation requires a passing check for the exact content hash. The
        previous active version remains available for rollback only while its
        check is valid. If no retained version has a valid check, the operation
        is unavailable and the reason is escalated; the factory does not guess.
        Re-running a check creates a new activation record even when content is
        unchanged. Credential rotation invalidates affected checks through a
        credential-to-version index.
    checkSpecification:
        Each operation declares a check: name, inputs, expected result, timeout,
        cost ceiling, cleanup, criticality, TTL, and manual eligibility. The
        preferred check exercises the wiring end to end in an isolated sandbox
        with disposable targets and no production or customer effects.
        If no harmless automated check is possible, a manual check may be
        approved per operation by the engineer. The engineer records its
        result, evidence, actor, and expiry. An agent cannot declare its own
        operation manual.
    checkExecution:
        Checks run on registration, on wiring or credential change, after
        repair, at the per-operation TTL, and after a related failure. Related
        means operation plus route plus wiring version plus error signature.
        Due checks trigger revalidation but stay active through a grace period;
        expired checks deactivate the wiring. A check that cannot run because of
        capacity, exhausted allowance, or provider outage reports an
        unavailable-check blocker and does not silently drift from due to
        expired.
        Checks are serialized per wiring version and activation uses
        compare-and-swap. Check cost is reserved under the control budget, in
        currency and usage units, with a per-interval cap. A check exceeding its
        declared ceiling needs engineer approval. Cleanup failure is a failed
        check.
    checkFailure:
        Classification is normative and stored on the check record. Provider 401
        or 403 is auth; 429 is rate limit or exhausted allowance depending on
        allowance data; 5xx, network failure, and timeout after a previously
        passing check are outage; schema, parse, exit-code, or response-shape
        mismatch is wiring; missing configuration is config; ambiguity is
        unknown and does not auto-repair. A 429 with stale or missing allowance
        data is treated as throttling with an immediate refresh; repeated
        unexplained refusals escalate.
        An exhausted allowance makes the check unavailable, not failed: it does
        not deactivate wiring or start repair, and its TTL does not expire into
        deactivation while blocked.
        A failed check stops new dispatch on that wiring; in-flight work
        continues pinned. If a critical operation has no available route,
        factory dispatch pauses with a blocker. Repair is scoped, authorized,
        and budgeted. Repeated outages escalate.
